# IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md — SkillGap Implementation Roadmap

This plan is derived strictly from `PRD.md` and `ARCHITECTURE.md` and is written to be executable by an AI coding agent (or a small student team) working through the repository sequentially. It assumes the Next.js modular-monolith stack, Prisma schema, and layered `route → service → repository` pattern defined in `ARCHITECTURE.md`.

---

> **Implementation scope guard:** Build only features marked **P0 / MVP** as mandatory. P1 features may be implemented only after all P0 acceptance criteria pass. P2/P3 and explicitly post-MVP/future features must not be implemented as part of the MVP unless this plan is deliberately re-scoped. Internal data needed to support future features may exist, but user-facing behavior and implementation work should remain within the stated priority boundary.


## 1. Development Strategy

**Order of implementation: foundation-first, then vertical feature slices, then hardening.**

1. **Foundation first (Phases 0–2):** project scaffolding, the full Prisma schema (all entities up front, even ones only used in later phases), and the shared backend skeleton (layered structure, error mapping, Zod validation conventions, the `AIProvider` interface, the background job runner). Building the schema once — instead of growing it phase by phase — avoids repeated destructive migrations and lets every later phase write against a stable data model.
2. **Vertical feature slices (Phases 3–8):** each phase after the foundation delivers one feature **end-to-end** — database access → service logic → API routes → UI — rather than "all backend, then all frontend." This matches how the PRD's user flows are structured (Flow A/B/C) and means the project is demoable after every phase, which matters for a hackathon/student timeline where the project may need to be shown at any checkpoint.
3. **Hardening last (Phases 9–10):** cross-cutting testing, error-state polish, rate limiting, and deployment are consolidated at the end because they depend on every feature already existing, and because doing them once at the end avoids re-testing the same flow repeatedly as it changes.

**Why not pure horizontal (all backend, then all frontend)?** The reference architecture's own principle — deterministic, unit-testable business logic in the service layer, AI only for extraction/generation — is easiest to verify when each service ships with the minimal UI needed to exercise it manually, rather than accumulating five backend services with no way to click through them until the very end.

**Why database schema is front-loaded rather than grown per phase:** every phase from Phase 3 onward reads or writes entities owned by other phases (e.g., the Roadmap Engine in Phase 8 reads `Evidence` from Phase 6 and `GitHubRepository` from Phase 7). Defining the full schema in Phase 1 — informed by the ER diagram in `ARCHITECTURE.md` §7 — means later phases only ever run additive migrations (new columns/indexes), never structural ones.

